The uniform root enclosures and complete interval exhaustion in \hyperref[app:source-neighborhood]{Appendix D} establish exactly 48 rays. On their labels, the interval overlap tests retain a graph \(K_{4,4}\sqcup20K_2\): one complete bipartite graph with four vertices on each side, together with twenty disjoint pairs. It has 48 vertices and 36 possible orthogonality edges. Every actual orthogonal pair must be an edge; a retained edge need not be orthogonal. The graph is triangle-free, and every nonedge has overlap modulus \(>1/120\). Three distinct labels therefore contain such a nonedge, while equal labels have overlap modulus one.

Each column of \(K/\sqrt6\) is one of these rays, up to its unrestricted global phase. Form a graph on the six column positions, joining two positions only when their labels are distinct and adjacent in the retained graph. Repeated labels introduce no triangle. A triangle-free graph on six vertices has at most nine edges: for every edge \(uv\), \(\deg(u)+\deg(v)\le6\). Summing over edges and applying Cauchy gives
\begin{equation}
4e^2\le6\sum_v\deg(v)^2\le36e,
\end{equation}
so \(e\le9\) when \(e>0\); the zero-edge case is immediate. At least six of the fifteen column pairs thus have overlap modulus \(>1/120\). Writing the unit columns as \(v_j\), including repetitions,
\begin{equation}
\|K^*K-6I\|_F^2
=36\cdot2\sum_{j<k}|\langle v_j,v_k\rangle|^2
>36\cdot2\cdot\frac6{120^2}=\frac3{100}.
\end{equation}
Division by 36 gives the normalized gap. A third orthonormal basis would have zero Gram defect, contradicting this strict bound. The supplementary character sums are treated separately in \hyperref[sec:source-marks]{Appendix D.1}. \(\square

In this algebraic argument, row and column permutations and diagonal rescalings by nonzero elements of \(F\) preserve the entry-product condition for \ensuremath{C} and its inverse, and preserve nonvanishing of all 3-minors. Normalize the first of the four selected rows to all ones. On column pair j, write the next selected row as \ensuremath{(r_{j},r_{j})}. Invertibility gives, for distinct rows \ensuremath{h,l},

\begin{equation}
\sum_m\frac{C_{hm}}{C_{\ell m}}=0\qquad(h\ne\ell),
\end{equation}

because \(T_{m\ell}=1/(6C_{\ell m})\). Applied in both directions to the first two selected rows, this says \(\sum_jr_j=0\) and \(\sum_jr_j^{-1}=0\). The three nonzero numbers \ensuremath{r_{j}} are consequently the roots of \(X^3-r_0r_1r_2\). Since characteristic is not three, they are distinct. Scale and order so \(r_j=1,\omega,\omega^2\), with \ensuremath{\omega} a primitive cube root of unity.

Write the other two selected rows on pair \(j\) as \((a_j,b_j)\) and \((s_ja_j,s_jb_j)\). Applying the same two row-ratio identities shows that, after scaling the fourth row, \(s_j=r_j\) or \(s_j=r_j^{-1}\). Indeed every permutation of the three cube roots is a scalar multiple of either of those two indexed triples.

Put \(A_j=a_j+b_j\). Orthogonality in the row-ratio sense between the first two rows and the next two gives

\begin{equation}
\sum_jA_j=0,\qquad\sum_j\frac{A_j}{r_j}=0,\qquad\sum_js_jA_j=0,\qquad\sum_j\frac{s_j}{r_j}A_j=0.
\end{equation}

For either choice of \ensuremath{s}, these include all three Fourier equations on three distinct cube roots, whose coefficient matrix is invertible. Therefore \(A_j=0\) and \(b_j=-a_j\).

Take either of the two remaining rows and write its entries on pair j as \ensuremath{(p_{j}^+,p_{j}^-)}. Its row-ratio equations against the first four rows imply

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}p_j^+&=hr_j^2+\ell a_js_j^2,\\ p_j^-&=hr_j^2-\ell a_js_j^2.\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

for scalars \(h,\ell\). This follows by applying the same invertible three-point Fourier matrix separately to \(p_j^++p_j^-\) and \((p_j^+-p_j^-)/a_j\). If \(\ell=0\), that row together with the first two rows has proportional columns on each pair, giving a zero 3-minor. If \(h=0\), the same is true for that row together with the third and fourth selected rows. Thus the nonzero-three-minor hypothesis forces \(h\) and \(\ell\) both nonzero.

Set \(D_j=h^2r_j^4-\ell^2a_j^2s_j^4\). Every \ensuremath{D_{j}} is nonzero because it is \ensuremath{p_{j}^+p_{j}^-} and all entries are nonzero. The reversed row-ratio equations, using the first and second rows divided by this remaining row, give

\begin{equation}
\sum_j\frac{hr_j^2}{D_j}=0,\qquad\sum_j\frac{r_jhr_j^2}{D_j}=0.
\end{equation}

Thus \(hr_j^2/D_j=\gamma r_j\) for one nonzero \ensuremath{\gamma}. As \(r_j^4=r_j\) and \(s_j^4=s_j\), it follows that

\begin{equation}
a_j^2s_j=\kappa r_j
\end{equation}

for a nonzero \ensuremath{\kappa}. Hence \((a_js_j^2/r_j^2)^2=\kappa\). Over the algebraically closed field choose \ensuremath{\eta} with \(\eta^2=\kappa\). Swap the two columns within each pair when necessary, which replaces \ensuremath{a_{j}} by \ensuremath{-a_{j}} without changing \ensuremath{r_{j}} or \ensuremath{s_{j}}, to obtain

\begin{equation}
a_j=\eta\frac{r_j^2}{s_j^2}\qquad\text{for every }j.
\end{equation}

If \(s_j=r_j\), then \(a_j=\eta\); the first and third selected rows are proportional on the three first columns of the pairs. If \(s_j=r_j^{-1}\), then \(a_j=\eta r_j\) and \(s_ja_j=\eta\); the first and fourth selected rows are proportional on those three columns. In either case, adding any other row gives a zero positional three-minor. This contradiction proves the lemma. Applying it to \(C^{\mathsf T}\) proves the transposed assertion. All denominators used were nonzero, and only invertibility of two and three and the matrix hypotheses were used.
